\[\n\\boxed{L_{1}:\\; y=1},\\qquad \n\\boxed{L_{2}:\\; x=1},\\qquad \n\\boxed{L_{3}:\\; x+y=4}.\n\\]\n\n* \\(L_{1}\\) is horizontal, hence parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis.  \n* \\(L_{2}\\) is vertical, hence parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis.  \n* \\(L_{3}\\) has slope \\(-1\\), hence is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nThus none of the three lines is sunny, so the number of sunny lines is \\(k=0\\).\n\nNow we verify that every point of \\(P\\) lies on at least one of the three lines.\n\n| Point \\((a,b)\\) | Lies on which line(s)? | Verification |\n|----------------|-----------------------|--------------|\n| \\((1,1)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{2}\\) | \\(y=1\\) and \\(x=1\\) both hold |\n| \\((1,2)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) | \\(x=1\\) holds |\n| \\((1,3)\\) | \\(L_{2}\\) and \\(L_{3}\\) | \\(x=1\\) holds; \\(x+y=1+3=4\\) holds |\n| \\((2,1)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) | \\(y=1\\) holds |\n| \\((2,2)\\) | \\(L_{3}\\) | \\(x+y=2+2=4\\) holds |\n| \\((3,1)\\) | \\(L_{1}\\) and \\(L_{3}\\) | \\(y=1\\) holds; \\(x+y=3+1=4\\) holds |\n\nAll six points are covered, and the three lines are distinct. Hence the required configuration exists with exactly \\(k=0\\) sunny lines.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, the value }k=0\\text{ is attainable.}}\n\\]\n\n\n\n---\n\n## CRITICAL: Few-Shot Calibration Examples (2025-12-28 Data Leakage Fix)\n\n**These examples show you how to apply the decision rule above. Study them carefully before verifying the solution.**\n\n**Example 1: Justification Gap (NOT Critical Error)**\n*This example shows presentation issues that should be classified as Justification Gaps.*\n\nProblem: \"Find all prime numbers p such that p² + 2 is also prime.\"\n\nSolution excerpt: \"For p=3, we have 3²+2=11 which is prime. For p>3, we have p≡1 or 2 (mod 3), so p²≡1 (mod 3), thus p²+2≡0 (mod 3). Since p²+2 is divisible by 3 and p²+2>3 for p>3, it must be composite. Final answer: p=3.\"\n\n**Applying the Decision Rule:**\n1. Check final answer: p=3 ✓ CORRECT\n2. Check construction