#### 1.  Collinearity of the six points  \n\nCompute the slope of the line through any two distinct points:\n\n| Pair | Slope |\n|------|-------|\n| \\(AB,AC,BC\\) | \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) |\n| \\(AD,AF,DF\\) | \\(0\\) (horizontal) |\n| \\(BD,CE,EF\\) | \\(-1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)) |\n| \\(AE\\) | \\(1\\) |\n| \\(BF\\) | \\(-\\tfrac12\\) |\n| \\(CD\\) | \\(-2\\) |\n| all other pairs | either vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\) |\n\nHence any three collinear points among \\(\\{A,B,C,D,E,F\\}\\) lie on a line whose slope is \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nConsequently:\n\n* **Lemma 1.** Every *sunny* line (i.e. a line whose slope is not \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\)) contains **at most two** of the six points.  \n\n* **Lemma 2.** The only unordered pairs of points that can lie on a sunny line are  \n\\[\n\\{A,E\\},\\qquad \\{B,F\\},\\qquad \\{C,D\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The non‑sunny line  \n\nAmong the three required lines exactly one is non‑sunny.  \nA non‑sunny line can be of three types:\n\n* vertical: \\(x=c\\);\n* horizontal: \\(y=c\\);\n* slope \\(-1\\): \\(x+y=c\\).\n\nA non‑sunny line may contain either two or three of the six points.  \nAll possibilities are listed below.\n\n| Type | Equation | Points on the line |\n|------|----------|--------------------|\n| vertical | \\(x=1\\) | \\(A,B,C\\) |\n| vertical | \\(x=2\\) | \\(D,E\\) |\n| vertical | \\(x=3\\) | \\(F\\) |\n| horizontal | \\(y=1\\) | \\(A,D,F\\) |\n| horizontal | \\(y=2\\) | \\(B,E\\) |\n| horizontal | \\(y=3\\) | \\(C\\) |\n| slope \\(-1\\) | \\(x+y=2\\) | \\(A\\) |\n| slope \\(-1\\) | \\(x+y=3\\) | \\(B,D\\) |\n| slope \\(-1\\) | \\(x+y=4\\) | \\(C,E,F\\) |\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Case analysis  \n\nWe examine separately the two possibilities for the number of points on the non‑sunny line.\n\n---\n\n##### **Case 1.** The non‑sunny line contains **three** points.  \n\nThe only such lines are \\(x=1\\), \\(y=1\\) and \\(x+y=4\\).  \nThe remaining three points are respectively  \n\n* after \\(x=1\\): \\(\\{D,E,F\\}\\);\n* after \\(y=1\\): \\(\\{B,C,E\\}\\);\n* after \\(x+y=4\\): \\(\\{A,B,D\\}\\).\n\nIn each of these triples every pair has slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) (see the table of slopes).  \nTherefore **no** pair can lie on a sunny line, contradicting the requirement that the two remaining lines be sunny.  \nHence a configuration with a three‑point non‑sunny line is impossible.\n\n---\n\n##### **Case 2.** The non‑sunny line contains **exactly two** points.  \n\nThe only such lines are  \n\n* \\(x=2\\)   (points \\(D,E\\));\n* \\(y=2\\)   (points \\(B,E\\));\n* \\(x+y=3\\) (points \\(B,D\\)).\n\nThe four points left uncovered must be covered by the two sunny lines.  \nBy Lemma 2 the only admissible sunny pairs are \\(\\{A,E\\},\\{B,F\\},\\{C,D\\}\\).  \nWe check each possibility:\n\n| Non‑sunny line | Remaining points | Possible sunny pairs among them | Result |\n|----------------|------------------|--------------------------------|--------|\n| \\(x=2\\) ( \\(D,E\\) ) | \\(\\{A,B,C,F\\}\\) | only \\(\\{B,F\\}\\) is allowed; leaves \\(\\{A,C\\}\\) (vertical) → impossible |\n| \\(y=2\\) ( \\(B,E\\) ) | \\(\\{A,C,D,F\\}\\) | only \\(\\{C,D\\}\\) is allowed; leaves \\(\\{A,F\\}\\) (horizontal) → impossible |\n| \\(x+y=3\\) ( \\(B,D\\) ) | \\(\\{A,C,E,F\\}\\) | only \\(\\{A,E\\}\\) is allowed; leaves \\(\\{C,F\\}\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) → impossible |\n\nIn every sub‑case the four remaining points cannot be partitioned into two disjoint sunny pairs, contradicting the existence of two sunny lines.\n\nThus a configuration with a two‑point non‑sunny line is also impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nBoth possibilities for the non‑sunny line lead to contradictions.  \nTherefore **no** set of three distinct lines can cover the six required