}{k}\\,x + (k+1)\n        \\]\n        which passes through the points \\((1,k)\\) and \\((k,1)\\) **and** has slope \\(-\\frac{k-1}{k}\\neq -1,0,\\infty\\); therefore \\(L_k\\) is sunny.  \n\n        The line \\(L_k\\) also contains every point of the diagonal \\(D_k\\) except possibly the middle one when \\(k\\) is odd. That middle point is \\(\\bigl(\\frac{k+1}{2},\\frac{k+1}{2}\\bigr)\\); it lies on the sunny line \\(y=x\\), which is already present in the covering of \\(T_{k-1}\\) (the line \\(y=x\\) is part of the induction hypothesis for all \\(k\\ge 2\\)).  \n\n        Consequently all points of \\(T_k\\) are now covered by the \\(k-1\\) lines from the induction hypothesis together with the new line \\(L_k\\).  \n\n   Thus \\(T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) sunny lines for every \\(k\\ge 0\\).\n\n4. **Putting the pieces together.**  \n   For any prescribed \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)) we use the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines of Step\u202f1 and the \\(k\\) sunny lines obtained for \\(T_k\\) in Step\u202f3.  \n   The total number of lines is \\(n\\), exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.  \n\nHence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable, and no other values are possible.\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for all \\(k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) such that  \n\n1. \\(\\displaystyle\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\supseteq T_n\\);  \n2. exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An obvious upper bound\n\nA line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis is of the form \\(x=c\\) and contains all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate equals \\(c\\).  \nSince the first coordinates occurring 