eed to cover all integer points inside this triangle.\n\nNon-sunny lines can be vertical (x = constant), horizontal (y = constant), or diagonal (x+y = constant). These are lines parallel to the sides of the triangle (vertical side x=1, horizontal side y=1, and diagonal side x+y=n+1). So non-sunny lines are parallel to the sides of the triangle.\n\nThus the problem reduces to covering the integer points of a triangular lattice with lines parallel to the sides (non-sunny) and some other lines (sunny). The non-sunny lines are essentially \"grid lines\" of the triangular lattice.\n\nIf we use only non-sunny lines (parallel to sides), we would need at least n lines to cover all points: we could use vertical lines x=1,...,x=n (n lines) covering all points, but we need exactly n lines total. However, we need exactly 2 sunny lines, so we need to replace some of the vertical lines with sunny lines to reduce the number of non-sunny lines to n-2. The question is whether we can cover the triangle with n-2 lines parallel to sides and 2 other lines of arbitrary slope.\n\nThe solution claims that we cannot do it with only 2 sunny lines because after using n-2 vertical lines we have 3 points left that cannot be covered by 2 sunny lines. But perhaps we can use a different combination of non-sunny lines (some vertical, some horizontal, some diagonal) to reduce the uncovered set to something that can be covered by 2 sunny lines.\n\nLet's try to find a configuration for n=5 (or general n) with n-2 non-sunny lines that are not all vertical, but a mix, such that the uncovered points can be covered by 2 sunny lines.\n\nOne approach: Use n-2 non-sunny lines that are all parallel to one side (vertical) except maybe one diagonal that covers the outermost diagonal points, reducing the uncovered set.\n\nFor n=5, we have 3 non-sunny lines. Suppose we use vertical lines x=1 and x=2 (cover many points) and a diagonal line x+y=5 (covers points (1,4),(2,3),(3,2),(4,1)). Then uncovered points: Let's