with any sunny line through the single uncovered point \\((n,1)\\) (for instance \\(y=2x-(2n-1)\\)).  \n   *\\(k=3\\).*  The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   L_{1}:y=x,\\qquad\n   L_{2}:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\qquad\n   L_{3}:y=-2x+5\n   \\]\n\n   cover the triangle \\(T_{3}\\) (six points) and together with the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-3\\) give a configuration with exactly three sunny lines.\n\n5. **Inductive construction for all \\(k\\ge5\\).**  \n   We prove that for every integer \\(k\\ge5\\) the triangle  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{k}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  \n   The construction proceeds by induction on \\(k\\).\n\n   *Base \\(k=5\\).*  The five sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_{1}&:y=x,\\\\\n   L_{2}&:y=-2x+7,\\\\\n   L_{3}&:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac72,\\\\\n   L_{4}&:y=\\tfrac12x,\\\\\n   L_{5}&:y=-\\tfrac32x+7\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   are easily checked (substitution) to contain respectively  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &\\{(1,1),(2,2),(3,3)\\},\\\\\n   &\\{(1,5),(2,3),(3,1)\\},\\\\\n   &\\{(1,3),(3,2),(5,1)\\},\\\\\n   &\\{(2,1),(4,2)\\},\\\\\n   &\\{(2,4),(4,1)\\},\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   which together are exactly the ten points of \\(T_{5}\\).  Hence \\(T_{5}\\) is covered by five sunny lines.\n\n   *Inductive step.*  Assume that for some \\(k\\ge5\\) we have a family  \n   \\(\\mathcal L_{k}=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\) of sunny lines covering \\(T_{k}\\).  \n   Consider \\(T_{k+3}\\).  The set difference \\(T_{k+3}\\setminus T_{k}\\) consists of the three \u201couter\u2011layer\u2019\u2019 points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{k+3}=\\{(1,k+2),\\;(k+2,1),\\;(2,k+1)\\}.\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   (All other new points lie on the same rows or columns as points already in \\(T_{k}\\) and are therefore already covered.)  \n   The three points of \\(P_{k+3}\\) are pairwise non\u2011collinear with slopes