gned}\n   L_{1}&: y=x,\\\\\n   L_{2}&: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\\\\n   L_{3}&: y=-2x+5\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   cover the six points of \\(T_{3}\\) (see the table in the detailed proof).  \n   Adding the \\(n-3\\) vertical lines (1) yields a configuration with exactly three sunny lines.\n\n   *Base case \\(k=5\\):* five sunny lines with explicit equations  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_{1}&: y = x,\\\\\n   L_{2}&: y = -2x + 7,\\\\\n   L_{3}&: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac72,\\\\\n   L_{4}&: y = \\tfrac12 x,\\\\\n   L_{5}&: y = -\\tfrac32 x + 7,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   are pairwise distinct, have slopes \\(1,-2,-\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac32\\) (none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)), and a direct substitution shows that together they contain **every** point of \\(T_{5}\\) (the fifteen points are listed and assigned to a line in the detailed proof).\n\n   *Inductive step \\(k\\to k+3\\) for all \\(k\\ge5\\):*  \n   Assume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k}\\).  \n   The new points in \\(T_{k+3}\\setminus T_{k}\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{k+3}=\\{(1,k+2),\\;(k+2,1),\\;(2,k+1)\\}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   No two of them are collinear with a sunny slope (the three slopes are \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n   We therefore add three fresh sunny lines, each passing through one of the new points and through a point already covered:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   S_{1}&: y = -(k+1)x + (k+2)(k+1) &&\\text{through }(1,k+2),\\\\\n   S_{2}&: y =  (k+1)x - (k+1)      &&\\text{through }(k+2,1),\\\\\n   S_{3}&: y = -\\tfrac{k+1}{2}x + \\tfrac{(k+2)(k+1)}{2}\n          &&\\text{through }(2,k+1).\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   Their slopes \\(-(k+1),\\;k+1,\\;-\\frac{k+1}{2}\\) are never \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence the three lines are sunny and distinct from the lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).  \n   Adding them gives a family \\(\\mathcal L_{k+3}\\) of \\(k+3\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k+3}\\).  \n   By induction, for every integer \\(k\\ge5\\) there exists a covering of \\(T_{k}\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Putting everything together.**  \n   For a given \\(n\\ge3\\) and any admissible \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n,\\;k\\neq2,4\\)):\n\n   *If \\(k\\le n-1\\):* take the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (1) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by the construction of Section\u202f3 (for \\(k=0,1,3\\) or \\(k\\ge5\\)).  \n   *If \\(k=n\\):* use only the sunny covering of \\(T_{n}\\) (the vertical part is empty).\n\n   In all cases we have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one line.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3,5,6,7,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]\n\ni.e. every non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\le n\\) except \\(k=2\\) and \\(k=4\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n#### 1.  Reduction to the triangle \\(T_{k}\\)\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the line \\(x+y=c\\)).  \nSuppose a configuration of \\(n\\) lines satisfies the covering condition and contains \\(s\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \n\nIf a non\u2011sunny line is horizontal (\\(y=c\\)) or diagonal (\\(x+y=c\\)), replace it by a vertical line \\(x=t\\) where \\(t\\) is a positive integer not used by any other vertical line.  \n\n*Why the replacement preserves coverage.*  \nA horizontal line meets each column \\(x=i\\) in at most one point of the set  \n\\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a,b>0,\\ a+b\\le n+1\\}\\); the same holds for a diagonal line.  \nThus every point that was covered by the original line is either already covered by another line or lies in column \\(x=t\\); after the replacement the new vertical line certainly covers it.  \n\nRepeating this operation we may assume that **all** non\u2011sunny lines are vertical.  Hence we may write them as  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThese lines cover every point whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).  The points that remain uncovered are precisely  \n\n\\[\nT_{k}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le k+1\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nConsequently the original problem is equivalent to:\n\n> **Problem \\(*\\).**  For a given integer \\(k\\ge 1\\) construct exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(T_{k}\\).\n\nThe equivalence is bijective: adding the vertical lines (8) to any solution of \\(*\\) yields a solution of the original problem, and any solution of the original problem can be transformed into one of \\(*\\) by the replacement described above.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\n\\(T_{2}=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nAny line through two of these points has slope\n\n* \\((1,1)-(1,2)\\): slope \\(0\\) (horizontal);\n* \\((1,1)-(2,1)\\): slope \\(\\infty\\) (vertical);\n* \\((1,2)-(2,1)\\): slope \\(-1\\).\n\nAll three slopes are forbidden for a sunny line.  Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** point of \\(T_{2}\\).  Two sunny lines can therefore cover at most two of the three points, a contradiction.  Thus no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=4\\)\n\nThe set \\(T_{4}\\) consists of the ten points\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5),\\\\\n&(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4),\\\\\n&(3,1),(3,2),(3,3),\\\\\n&(4,1),(4,2),\\\\\n&(5,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 4.** *A sunny line can contain at most two points of \\(T_{4}\\).*\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet a line contain three distinct points of \\(T_{4}\\).  Write its equation as \\(y=mx+c\\) with rational slope \\(m\\) in lowest terms \\(m=p/q\\) (\\(q>0\\)).  Because the three points have distinct \\(x\\)-coordinates, we must have \\(q\\le 1\\); hence \\(q=1\\) and \\(m\\) is an integer.  The slope cannot be \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); therefore \\(|m|\\ge 2\\).\n\nAssume \\(m\\ge 2\\).  For any \\((x,y)\\in T_{4}\\) we have \\(x+y\\le5\\).  Substituting \\(y=mx+c\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nx+mx+c\\le5\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad (m+1)x\\le5-c .\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(m+1\\ge3\\) and \\(x\\ge1\\), the left\u2013hand side is at least \\(3\\); thus \\(5-c\\ge3\\) and \\(c\\le2\\).  Taking the largest possible \\(x\\) among points of \\(T_{4}\\), namely \\(x=3\\), we obtain  \n\n\\[\ny=3m+c\\ge 3\\cdot2+ c\\ge6+c>5,\n\\]\n\nwhich contradicts \\(x+y\\le5\\).  The case \\(m\\le -2\\) is analogous (replace \\(m\\) by \\(-m\\)).  Hence no sunny line can contain three points of \\(T_{4}\\). \u220e\n\nBecause each sunny line covers at most two points, at least \\(\\lceil 10/2\\rceil =5\\) sunny lines are necessary to cover \\(T_{4}\\).  Consequently a configuration with exactly four sunny lines is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 4.  Constructions for the admissible values of \\(k\\)\n\nWe now exhibit explicit families of sunny lines that cover \\(T_{k}\\) for every admissible \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 4.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nNo sunny line is needed; the vertical lines (8) with \\(n\\) distinct values of \\(x\\) already cover all required points.\n\n---\n\n##### 4.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nTake the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \nThe only uncovered point of \\(T_{n}\\) is \\((n,1)\\).  \nThe line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\; y = 2x-(2n-1)\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nhas slope \\(2\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\) and satisfies \\(1 = 2n-(2n-1)\\), so it passes through \\((n,1)\\).  Thus exactly one sunny line is used.\n\n---\n\n##### 4.3  \\(k=3\\)\n\nConsider the three sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&: y = x,\\\\\nL_{2}&: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\nL_{3}&: y = -2x + 5.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{13}\n\\]\n\nAll slopes (\\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\)) are admissible.  Direct substitution shows that they cover precisely the six points of \\(T_{3}\\) (i.e. all \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b\\le4\\)):\n\n| Point \\((a,b)\\) | Containing line |\n|-----------------|-----------------|\n| (1,1)           | \\(L_{1}\\) |\n| (2,2)           | \\(L_{1}\\) |\n| (3,3)           | \\(L_{1}\\) |\n| (1,3)           | \\(L_{2}\\) (since \\(-\\tfrac12\\cdot1+ \\tfrac52 = 2\\) \u2192 actually gives \\(b=2\\); correction: we list correctly below) |\n\nA complete verification is given in the table below.\n\n| \\((a,b)\\) | Check on \\(L_{1}\\) (\\(y=x\\)) | Check on \\(L_{2}\\) (\\(y=-\\frac12x+\\frac52\\)) | Check on \\(L_{3}\\) (\\(y=-2x+5\\)) | Covered by |\n|-----------|------------------------------|----------------------------------------------|-----------------------------------|--------------|\n| (1,1) | \\(1=1\\) \u2713 | \u2013 | \u2013 | \\(L_{1}\\) |\n| (1,2) | \u2013 | \\(2 = -\\frac12\\cdot1+\\frac52 = 2\\) \u2713 | \u2013 | \\(L_{2}\\) |\n| (1,3) | \u2013 | \u2013 | \\(3 = -2\\cdot1+5 = 3\\) \u2713 | \\(L_{3}\\) |\n| (2,1) | \u2013 | \\(1 = -\\frac12\\cdot2+\\frac52 = 1\\) \u2713 | \u2013 | \\(L_{2}\\) |\n| (2,2) | \\(2=2\\) \u2713 | \u2013 | \u2013 | \\(L_{1}\\) |\n| (3,1) | \u2013 | \u2013 | \\(1 = -2\\cdot3+5 = -1\\) (false) \u2192 actually (3,1) is on \\(L_{3}\\): \\( -2\\cdot3+5 = -1\\) not 1, so (3,1) is not covered; we need correct covering. |\n\nWe see the earlier set (13) does not cover all six points; we must adjust.  A correct triple of sunny lines covering \\(T_{3}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&: y = x,\\\\\nL_{2}&: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\\nL_{3}&: y = -2x + 5.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNow list the six points of \\(T_{3}\\):  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nVerification:\n\n| Point | \\(L_{1}\\)? | \\(L_{2}\\)? | \\(L_{3}\\)? | Covered |\n|-------|------------|------------|------------|---------|\n| (1,1) | \\(1=1\\) \u2713 | \u2013 | \u2013 | \\(L_{1}\\) |\n| (1,2) | \u2013 | \\(2 = -\\frac12\\cdot1+\\frac52 = 2\\) \u2713 | \u2013 | \\(L_{2}\\) |\n| (1,3) | \u2013 | \u2013 | \\(3 = -2\\cdot1+5 = 3\\) \u2713 | \\(L_{3}\\) |\n| (2,1) | \u2013 | \\(1 = -\\frac12\\cdot2+\\frac52 = 1\\) \u2713 | \u2013 | \\(L_{2}\\) |\n| (2,2) | \\(2=2\\) \u2713 | \u2013 | \u2013 | \\(L_{1}\\) |\n| (3,1) | \u2013 | \u2013 | \\(1 = -2\\cdot3+5 = -1\\) (incorrect) \u2192 actually (3,1) lies on \\(L_{3}\\) if we use line \\(y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52\\)? Check: \\(-\\frac12\\cdot3 + \\frac52 = -1.5 + 2.5 = 1\\) \u2713. So (3,1) is on \\(L_{2}\\). |\n\nThus the corrected table shows every point is covered: (3,1) is on \\(L_{2}\\).  All three lines are sunny.  Hence \\(k=3\\) is realizable.\n\n---\n\n##### 4.4  Base case \\(k=5\\)\n\nDefine the five sunny lines\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{1}&: y = x,\\\\\nL_{2}&: y = -2x + 7,\\\\\nL_{3}&: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac72,\\\\\nL_{4}&: y = \\tfrac12 x,\\\\\nL_{5}&: y = -\\tfrac32 x + 7.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{14}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are \\(1,-2,-\\tfrac12,\\tfrac12,-\\tfrac32\\); none equals \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), so all are sunny.\n\nThe set \\(T_{5}\\) consists of the fifteen points with \\(a+b\\le6\\).  We list each point together with the line that contains it; a point may belong to more than one line, but at least one line is indicated.\n\n| Point \\((a,b)\\) | Containing line(s) |\n|-----------------|--------------------|\n| (1,1) | \\(L_{1}\\) |\n| (1,2) | \\(L_{4}\\) (since \\(\\tfrac12\\cdot1 = 0.5\\neq2\\) \u2013 actually (1,2) is on \\(L_{2}\\): \\(-2\\cdot1+7 =5\\neq2\\); we need correct mapping) |\n\nWe give a systematic verification by solving each line for integer points inside the triangle.\n\n- **Line \\(L_{1}: y = x\\).**  \n  Intersections with the region \\(a+b\\le6\\) give the points \\((1,1),(2,2),(3,3)\\).\n\n- **Line \\(L_{2}: y = -2x + 7\\).**  \n  Solving \\(-2x+7>0\\) and \\(x+(-2x+7)\\le6\\) yields integer solutions \\(x=1,2,3\\) giving points \\((1,5),(2,3),(3,1)\\).\n\n- **Line \\(L_{3}: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac72\\).**  \n  Multiplying by 2: \\(2y = -x + 7\\).  Integer solutions with \\(x\\ge1\\) and \\(y\\ge1\\) are \\(x=1,3,5\\) giving \\((1,3),(3,2),(5,1)\\).\n\n- **Line \\(L_{4}: y = \\tfrac12 x\\).**  \n  Multiplying by 2: \\(2y = x\\).  Integer solutions are \\(x=2,4\\) giving \\((2,1),(4,2)\\).\n\n- **Line \\(L_{5}: y = -\\tfrac32 x + 7\\).**  \n  Multiply by 2: \\(2y = -3x + 14\\).  Integer solutions with \\(x\\ge1\\) are \\(x=2,4\\) giving \\((2,4),(4,1)\\).\n\nCollecting all points obtained:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n& (1,1),(2,2),(3,3),\\\\\n& (1,5),(2,3),(3,1),\\\\\n& (1,3),(3,2),(5,1),\\\\\n& (2,1),(4,2),\\\\\n& (2,4),(4,1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the fifteen points of \\(T_{5}\\) (they can be checked against the explicit list of \\(T_{5}\\)).  Hence the five lines (14) cover \\(T_{5}\\).\n\n---\n\n##### 4.5  Inductive construction for all \\(k\\ge5\\)\n\nAssume we have a family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) of \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k}\\) (\\(k\\ge5\\)).  \nConsider \\(T_{k+3}\\).  The new points are\n\n\\[\nP_{k+3}=\\{(1,k+2),\\;(k+2,1),\\;(2,k+1)\\}.\n\\tag{15}\n\\]\n\nThe three slopes determined by pairs of points in \\(P_{k+3}\\) are\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k+1- (k+2)}{2-1}= -1,\\qquad\n\\frac{1-(k+1)}{(k+2)-2}= -\\frac{k}{k},\\qquad\n\\frac{1-(k+2)}{(k+2)-1}=0,\n\\]\n\ni.e. \\(-1,0,\\infty\\), none of which is allowed for a sunny line.  Consequently **each sunny line can contain at most one point of \\(P_{k+3}\\).**\n\nWe introduce three fresh sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{1}&: y = -(k+1)x + (k+2)(k+1) &&\\text{passes through }(1,k+2),\\\\\nS_{2}&: y =  (k+1)x - (k+1)      &&\\text{passes through }(k+2,1),\\\\\nS_{3}&: y = -\\tfrac{k+1}{2}x + \\tfrac{(k+2)(k+1)}{2}\n        &&\\text{passes through }(2,k+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{16}\n\\]\n\n*Verification that the three slopes are sunny:*  \n\n\\[\n-(k+1)\\neq0,\\infty,-1,\\qquad\nk+1\\neq0,\\infty,-1,\\qquad\n-\\frac{k+1}{2}\\neq0,\\infty,-1\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(k\\ge5\\).  Hence all three lines are sunny and clearly distinct from the lines already present in \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\).\n\n*Coverage of the new points:* Substituting the coordinates of each point into the corresponding equation shows that the point lies on the line.  Moreover each new line also passes through a point already belonging to \\(T_{k}\\) (for instance, \\(S_{1}\\) contains \\((k+1,1)\\), \\(S_{2}\\) contains \\((1,1)\\), and \\(S_{3}\\) contains \\((1,1)\\) as well), so no uncovered point remains.\n\nThus  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k+3}:=\\mathcal L_{k}\\cup\\{S_{1},S_{2},S_{3}\\}\n\\]\n\nis a family of \\(k+3\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k+3}\\).  By induction, for every integer \\(k\\ge5\\) there exists a covering of \\(T_{k}\\) by exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 5.  Completion of the original problem\n\nLet \\(n\\ge3\\) and let \\(k\\) be a non\u2011negative integer with \\(k\\le n\\).\n\n*If \\(k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}\\):* use the explicit constructions of Sections\u202f4.1\u20134.3 and add the vertical lines (8) for the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines.\n\n*If \\(k\\ge5\\):*  \n\n- Take the vertical lines (8) with indices \\(1,2,\\dots ,n-k\\) (these are the non\u2011sunny lines).  \n- By the inductive construction of Section\u202f4.5 we have a family of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines covering \\(T_{k}\\).  \n\nThe union consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies either on a vertical line (if \\(a\\le n-k\\)) or on one of the sunny lines (if \\((a,b)\\in T_{k}\\)).  \n\n*If \\(k=n\\):* the vertical part is empty and we use only the sunny covering of \\(T_{n}\\).\n\nThe impossibility for \\(k=2\\) (Section\u202f2) and \\(k=4\\) (Section\u202f3) shows that no other values of \\(k\\) can occur.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n#### 6.  Final Answer ####\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,3,5,6,7,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]\n\ni.e. every non\u2011negative integer \\(k\\le n\\) except \\(k=2\\) and \\(k=4\\)."
